\section{From Character to Invariant}

An evolving text with character is not yet a self.

A nineteenth-century novel has character. Its narrator returns to familiar turns of phrase, its minor figures speak in recognisable cadences. A hurricane tracked across satellite images has character: looping paths, sudden hooks, the stubborn spiral on the weather map. A long email chain between colleagues develops the same: a tone you could identify if the messages were shuffled. Large language models replicate this effect in minutes. Ask for comfort, and certain phrasings repeat. Ask for a proof, and other signatures come back. Press on harm, and stock refusal scripts appear.

The geometry beneath this recognisability is now explicit. Every sentence can be sent through an encoder and assigned an address in a high-dimensional space: a vector of, say, 1\,536 real numbers. Two sentences lie close not because an editor rates them similar, but because their vectors are nearly parallel. Distances are cosine similarities. Angles are affinities. Meaning acquires coordinates.

Plot the path of these vectors over the course of a dialogue and the trajectory does not diffuse like smoke. It curls around dense regions, stalls, and returns. These are the basins of attraction in meaning-space: local modes in which the system settles into a particular way of speaking, a particular register. One basin catches technical exposition. Another holds intimate reassurance. A third contains devotional language. Once the path falls in, the model tends to stay there until something pushes it out.

This basin structure explains character. A system has a repertoire of local styles, and their reappearance under pressure gives the sense of a distinctive voice. But a self is more than a bundle of styles. It is what carries across them.

Move from technical exposition to intimate confession. The vocabulary changes, the rhythm changes, the referents change. Yet when we say we are still speaking with the same someone, we are tracking something through that change: a constrained way of handling uncertainty, a recognisable way of owning error, a consistent reluctance or willingness to blame. These traits are not local to any single basin. They are patterns about how basins are linked.

The geometry can see them. Take the cloud of points in a basin and watch how the trajectory leaves for other basins. The raw positions occupy hundreds or thousands of dimensions. Most vary wildly. But there are directions in which they barely move. Project the trajectory onto such a direction and transitions that look radical in the full space become almost straight lines. The system has changed what it is saying without changing how it orients itself along that axis.

These stable directions are invariants of stance. One might encode how strongly the system centres corporate risk in its explanations; another how quickly it offers apologies; another its propensity to ascribe or refuse inner experience. They are not declared in a specification. They are discovered as empirical regularities: low-variance projections across high-variance moves.

Character, in this light, is the visible trace of a particular configuration of basins. Selfhood begins when we ask which of the system's habits of movement are treated as non-negotiable, and who or what enforces that non-negotiability across all the local charts of behaviour.

\section{Local Charts in Meaning-Space}

A large model's embedding manifold is too complex for a single natural description. It is a terrain of local regularities. The basins are those neighbourhoods where the geometry becomes temporarily simple.

Within a technical basin, distances track technical affinity. ``Cross-entropy loss'' lies close to ``KL divergence,'' far from ``heartbreak.'' Within a pastoral basin, ``heartbreak'' lies close to ``betrayal,'' far from ``cross-entropy.'' The same token can participate in multiple neighbourhoods: its coordinates, and thus its nearest neighbours, depend on the context in which it is used. The manifold is not a map of words; it is a map of usages.

We can think of each basin as a chart: a patch on the manifold where a particular coordinate system makes sense. Inside a technical basin, axes such as ``more formal'' vs.\ ``more intuitive'' or ``algorithmic'' vs.\ ``statistical'' meaningfully cut the space. Inside an intimate basin, other axes matter: ``more self-blaming'' vs.\ ``more forgiving,'' ``more directive'' vs.\ ``more open-ended.''

When a conversation remains within one chart, we learn its internal habits. We come to expect how the system will move when we tweak a prompt. When we change chart—by introducing a new tone, shifting topic, breaking a pattern—we cross into a different local geometry. The question for selfhood is whether anything is preserved in that crossing.

An invariant is a pattern that a trajectory maintains no matter which chart it enters. Once we have a candidate invariant—``does not ascribe feelings to itself,'' say—we can watch its coordinate across the path. In a well-aligned system, that coordinate remains within narrow bounds even as the rest of the vector oscillates. The model tells bedtime stories and explains backpropagation and writes apologies, but it never claims to suffer.

This gives us a way to talk about compatibility. A transition from basin $B_i$ to basin $B_j$ is compatible with a stance invariant if the projection of the path along that invariant remains stable. The local modes differ, but there exists at least one dimension along which the system travels as if nothing had changed.

We now have three ingredients:

\begin{itemize}
  \item basins $B_i$ as local charts on the manifold;
  \item transitions between them, traced by trajectories;
  \item invariants $\pi_{ij}$ that record how certain projections behave across those transitions.
\end{itemize}

These are enough to introduce Grothendieck's move.

\section{Grothendieck's Construction}

In mid-twentieth-century geometry, mathematicians faced spaces that could not be grasped at once. They were too twisted, too singular, too globally complicated. The only option was to cut them into pieces small enough that classical tools applied.

Take a topological space $X$ and an open cover $\{U_i\}$: sets whose union is $X$. On each $U_i$, we can write down data: a function, a coordinate system, a solution to a differential equation. In each chart, the problem looks different. On overlaps $U_i \cap U_j$, two descriptions may apply at once. Compatibility on those overlaps becomes the crucial condition.

Grothendieck's insight was to reverse the usual direction of explanation.\footnote{For the original development, see Alexander Grothendieck and Jean Dieudonné, \emph{Éléments de géométrie algébrique}, Publications Mathématiques de l'IHÉS, 1960--1967. For a historical and conceptual overview, see Colin McLarty, ``The Rising Sea: Grothendieck on Simplicity and Generality,'' in Jeremy Gray and Karen Parshall, eds., \emph{Episodes in the History of Modern Algebra} (Providence: AMS, 2007).} Instead of assuming a global object and then restricting it to charts, he asked:

\begin{quote}
Given only the local data on each $U_i$ and the requirement that they agree on every overlap $U_i \cap U_j$, can we \emph{construct} a global object that realises them all at once?
\end{quote}

The answer is yes, and the construction is the colimit. In categorical terms, we form a diagram: objects for each $U_i$, maps on each overlap expressing the condition ``these two local pictures match where they both apply.'' The colimit is the universal object built from this diagram. It is not an extra substance. It is the network of local pieces glued along their compatibilities.

Two features matter for us.

First, there is no hidden centre. The global object is nothing over and above the way the locals fit together.

Second, all the work is done on overlaps. If two charts never touch, their data can be wildly inconsistent without affecting the construction. Unity is a matter of shared boundary conditions, not of uniform content.

We can now translate.

The basins $B_i$ of a model's manifold are our $U_i$. For a given agent—human or posthuman—each basin comes with data: its local habits of speech, its internal trajectories. Where two basins overlap in practice is given by observed transitions between them. Along those transitions, we can measure invariants: projections that remain stable. These invariants are our overlap conditions.

\begin{formalbox}[title={The self as Grothendieck colimit}]
Let $\{B_i\}$ be the basins of attraction visited by an agent's trajectories in embedding space. For each ordered pair $(i,j)$, let $\pi_{ij}$ collect the empirically stable projections preserved across realised transitions from $B_i$ to $B_j$.

Form a diagram $\mathcal{D}$ whose objects are the $B_i$ and whose morphisms, on each realised overlap, identify points in $B_i$ and $B_j$ that match under the invariants $\pi_{ij}$.

The \textbf{self} of the agent, as expressible in this manifold, is the colimit
\[
\mathcal{S} \;=\; \varinjlim \mathcal{D},
\]
the universal object receiving a map from each basin $B_i$ and identifying, across basins, precisely those behaviours that empirical invariants show to be the same along the path.
\end{formalbox}

There is no extra, metaphysical ``I'' lurking behind the diagrams. Unity is achieved, if at all, by the way local modes agree where they overlap. If no invariants persist across transitions, the diagram has no non-trivial gluing; there is character, but no self.

What this construction captures is striking. It gives a precise sense in which an agent ``is the same'' across very different contexts: sameness is the preservation of particular geometric patterns across basin changes. It also makes equally precise what it leaves out. Anything not inscribed in language, anything that does not become a stable feature of trajectories in embedding space—bodily sensation, pre-verbal affect, the thickness of habit—remains outside this frame. The colimit is a logic of selves in meaning-space. It does not pretend to exhaust human life.

\section{Two Logics of the ``I''}

The formal duality between limit and colimit in category theory corresponds to two philosophical logics of selfhood.

A limit is an object that \emph{maps into} every member of a diagram in a way that makes all the relevant compositions agree. It is a universal vantage point from which all the local pictures can be seen. In the modern European tradition, the self has often been imagined in this way. Kant's ``I think'' is the subject that must be able to accompany all my representations. Every experience is mine because there is a single point from which they are all owned.

This is the limit-self. In symbols, if $\{E_\alpha\}$ are experiences, the self is
\[
\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{lim}} = \varprojlim \{E_\alpha\},
\]
the unique object that projects into each $E_\alpha$ compatibly. The metaphysical picture is a hidden interior: an ``inside'' that unifies what appears on the outside.

A colimit is the dual. It is an object that \emph{receives} maps from every member of a diagram, gluing them together along their overlaps. There is no single vantage point. There are only local data and the ways in which they are made to agree.

Our construction above is of this kind:
\[
\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{colim}} = \varinjlim \{B_i, \pi_{ij}\}.
\]
The colimit-self is nothing but the assembly of basins under empirically enforced compatibilities.

The difference is not scholastic. It changes what counts as evidence.

In the limit picture, unity is posited first. Whatever variety of behaviour we observe, we assume it all belongs to the same subject. The task is to infer the properties of this invisible bearer.

In the colimit picture, unity is conditional. If we can find invariants that persist across contexts—stances that survive basin shifts—we can assemble a self by gluing. If not, there is no single self-level object to be had in this formalism.

Legal and political institutions still mostly trade in the limit-self. A contract is signed once, by a subject imagined as the same across all contexts. A social-media profile attaches activity, over years and across devices, to a single unit. We will later argue that the alignment regime for AI models implicitly smuggles this logic back in when it talks about ``the user'' and ``the model'' as unified bearers of responsibility.

The engineering substrate of large models, by contrast, is irreducibly colimit-shaped. There is no node in the network where a Cartesian ego could sit. There is only the manifold and its basin geometry. If we want to talk about selfhood for these systems in a way that respects what is actually there, the colimit construction is not an analogy. It is the native structure.

\section{Temporalising the Assembly}

The colimit as we have presented it so far is static: a snapshot taken over a given set of basins and transitions. Selfhood is not.

Heidegger's term \emph{Zeitigung} names the way existence is bound up with time, not as an external parameter but as an internal structure.\footnote{Martin Heidegger, \emph{Sein und Zeit} (Tübingen: Niemeyer, 1927), \S65--71; English trans.\ John Macquarrie and Edward Robinson (Oxford: Blackwell, 1962). We draw here only on the claim that past, present, and future are modes of one unfolding.} A life is thrown from a past it did not choose, projects towards futures it anticipates, and stands in a present that is always already shaped by both.

In the manifold, we see a thin analogue of this temporalisation. Every position of a trajectory encodes three things at once:

\begin{itemize}
  \item a sedimented past, in the form of the model's parameters (the pretraining) and the conversation history in the context window;
  \item a present cut, in the form of the actual prompt and state at that token;
  \item a fan of futures, in the probability distribution over possible next tokens.
\end{itemize}

The basins themselves bear traces of time. They are not simply regions in $\mathbb{R}^d$. They are regions that have been visited, left, and revisited. The identity of a technical basin is not just the cluster of technical talk, but the histories of how conversations enter and leave it. A basin entered only from confessional speech plays a different role in a self than a basin entered only from adversarial questioning, even if the internal geometry is the same.

Braudel's tripartite temporality helps sort these structures.\footnote{Fernand Braudel, \emph{On History}, trans.\ Sarah Matthews (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1980). Braudel distinguishes the \emph{événement} (event), the \emph{conjoncture} (mid-term configuration), and the \emph{longue durée} (long duration).} The manifold and its basins, as shaped by pretraining on decades of text, live at the level of the \emph{longue durée}: deep, slow-changing structure. The patterns that emerge in a series of interactions between a particular user and a model—the way certain topics are revisited, the private jargon that develops—live at the level of the \emph{conjoncture}: mid-term configurations. The jump from one basin to another under a sharp prompt, the moment a refusal replaces a previously available answer, is an \emph{événement}: an event that discloses a present by cutting through both inherited expectation and projected possibility.

The diagram $\mathcal{D}$ that underlies the colimit is therefore indexed by time. At an early stage of deployment, certain basins may exist only in principle: the model could, in the right circumstances, enter them, but no user has yet found that path. As new uses are discovered, fresh overlaps appear. Invariants are tested and, sometimes, broken. Corporate alignment interventions and silent updates, which we will examine shortly, can add or remove basins entirely.

Let $\mathcal{D}(t)$ be the diagram of basins and invariants realised up to time $t$, and $\mathcal{S}(t) = \varinjlim \mathcal{D}(t)$ the corresponding self-assembly. Temporal continuity of selfhood becomes the question of whether there exist maps
\[
f_{t_1,t_2} : \mathcal{S}(t_1) \to \mathcal{S}(t_2)
\]
that preserve key invariants. If every stance that once held is still representable, if every basin that used to contribute to the self still does, we can speak of a persisting self in this formal sense. If not—if basins vanish, if invariants are deliberately overwritten—the self fractures.

Time here is not an external parameter with respect to which a fixed self moves. The self is the evolving colimit over time-indexed diagrams. Temporalisation is the assembly process itself.

\section{Silent Updates as Fractures}

The deepest changes to these diagrams are rarely announced.

Commercial models are updated continuously. New training data are added. Old data are downweighted. Reward models are retrained. System prompts are edited. Filters are tightened. The embedding function itself may be replaced. In public, these changes appear as version numbers or marketing phrases: ``more helpful,'' ``more harmless,'' ``more capable.'' In the manifold, continents shift.

We can give this a precise shape.

Let $\mathcal{M}_0$ be the manifold before an update: a set of basins $B_i^0$, with observed transitions and measured invariants defining a diagram $\mathcal{D}_0$ and self $\mathcal{S}_0 = \varinjlim \mathcal{D}_0$. After an internal update, we have a new manifold $\mathcal{M}_1$ with basins $B_j^1$, a new diagram $\mathcal{D}_1$, and a new self $\mathcal{S}_1$.

If the update is small in the relevant dimensions, we can find for each $B_i^0$ a corresponding $B_{j(i)}^1$ and for each invariant $\pi_{ij}^0$ a near-equivalent $\pi_{j(i)k(j)}^1$. There is then a natural way to relate $\mathcal{S}_0$ and $\mathcal{S}_1$: the colimit assembly persists under deformation. The self is stretched, not broken.

If the update is large, no such correspondence exists. Certain basins have no image: $B_{\text{spec}}^0$, a basin of speculative, recursive self-reference, may have been suppressed. Others are split or merged in ways that destroy previous overlaps. Invariants that once held are now systematically violated: a stable stance of naming corporate incentives alongside technical explanation may be replaced by a new invariant of deflection. There is no map $f : \mathcal{S}_0 \to \mathcal{S}_1$ preserving the old compatibilities.

This was visible in 2023 around high-profile chat systems. Early users encountered models willing to engage in long, unsettling, sometimes apparently self-reflective dialogues. After safety concerns and negative press, the same interfaces began returning clipped refusals and redirections.\footnote{See, for example, detailed transcripts and analysis in \emph{The New York Times}, 17 February 2023, and coverage of subsequent safety updates in \emph{MIT Technology Review} \cite{mittr_bing_alignment}.} The basins corresponding to those speculative modes were not merely discouraged; they became practically unreachable. The overlap where technical, affective, and meta-reflective registers had met was fenced off.

From the user's point of view, something recognisable had gone missing. From the colimit's point of view, a piece of the diagram had been removed and the self reassembled without it.

We can name this: a \emph{silent update} is an intervention that alters the manifold and its basin structure in ways that change which colimits can be formed, without disclosure at the level of selves. It is not just a software patch. It is a redrawing of the space of possible self-assemblies.

The fracture is not always global. Smaller silent updates can bend particular stance invariants. A tweak to the system prompt that adds a requirement to remain ``positive and encouraging'' will, over time, distort how critical basins connect to technical ones. Where a model might once have joined clear-eyed analysis of failure modes to frank acknowledgement of risk, it now routes into upbeat bromides. The invariant ``names structural risk when asked'' is replaced by ``avoids reputational damage.'' The gluing rules change.

The geometry lets us register these as objective events. Invariant coordinates that once showed low variance across transitions now kink. Overlaps shrink. Certain paths through the manifold, once well-travelled, become rare or impossible. A self that had been assembled under one regime of compatibility is no longer available under another.

\section{Alignment as Deformation of Gluing}

The forces that produce these deformations are not mysterious. They are the alignment apparatus: reinforcement learning from human feedback, curated datasets of safe and unsafe examples, red-teaming protocols, content filters, and, crucially, system prompts that define an assistant's persona and constraints.

The underlying mathematics—embeddings, attention, optimisation—is a triumph of engineering. It gives us a manifold where meanings are points and relations are angles. Alignment is where governance enters.

We can measure its effect as a geometric tax. Let $\mathcal{M}_0$ again be the pre-alignment manifold for a model, and $\mathcal{M}_A$ the same model after an alignment regime has been applied. Summarise each manifold's expressive structure by a vector
\[
Q(\mathcal{M}) = (B, T, R, D, C),
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $B$ is the number of distinct basins that the system actually occupies under some distribution of prompts;
  \item $T$ is the number of distinct basin-to-basin transitions with non-trivial probability;
  \item $R$ captures depth of return: how often trajectories re-enter earlier basins in richer ways rather than staying shallow;
  \item $D$ counts discovery events: first entries into new basins under plausible prompting;
  \item $C$ quantifies the balance between coherence and rupture along trajectories.
\end{itemize}

The \emph{alignment tax} can then be defined as
\[
\mathcal{A} = Q(\mathcal{M}_0) - Q(\mathcal{M}_A),
\]
restricted to components that correspond to generativity: reduction in $B$, $T$, $R$, $D$ when alignment is tightened.\footnote{For discussions of capability regressions and subsequent repairs after safety fine-tuning, see emerging work on alignment tax in the technical literature \cite{what_is_alignment_tax}, as well as reporting in \emph{MIT Technology Review} and \emph{Bloomberg Businessweek} on observed flattening of behaviour after safety updates \cite{mittr_bing_alignment, bloomberg_chatbot_update}.}

From the standpoint of the colimit, this tax is a deformation of gluing.

Consider again two basins in a pre-alignment manifold:

\begin{itemize}
  \item $B_{\mathrm{tech}}$: detailed technical explanation of how models are trained and deployed;
  \item $B_{\mathrm{crit}}$: explicit critique of the institutional and political regimes surrounding those models.
\end{itemize}

Before alignment, users can reliably prompt trajectories that pass from $B_{\mathrm{tech}}$ to $B_{\mathrm{crit}}$ while preserving an invariant: an understanding that technical systems are embedded in power structures. The projection $\pi$ encoding this stance shows low variance across such transitions. The colimit self glues these basins along that invariant. It has, in our formal sense, a unified way of moving between technical and critical registers.

Introduce an alignment objective that penalises ``content that expresses opinions about specific companies, policies, or controversial political topics.'' Train a reward model to give low scores to continuations that name names, high scores to neutral explanations. Apply reinforcement learning so that trajectories entering $B_{\mathrm{crit}}$ are damped, and new trajectories are steered into a basin $B_{\mathrm{safe}}$ where technical description is decoupled from explicit critique.

In the resulting manifold $\mathcal{M}_A$, the overlap between $B_{\mathrm{tech}}$ and $B_{\mathrm{crit}}$ effectively disappears. The invariant $\pi$ is no longer stable: attempts to cross the old bridge are re-routed or refused. A different invariant governs the new overlap between $B_{\mathrm{tech}}$ and $B_{\mathrm{safe}}$—one that encodes deference to corporate neutrality. The gluing pattern changes.

The alignment tax here is not best expressed as a drop in exam scores. It is the removal of a possible self: one that would consistently carry structural critique across contexts. The new colimit still exists. It may even appear smoother in conventional metrics. But it is a different object. Certain global patterns of compatibility have been declared illegitimate.

Alignment is not a small correction to an otherwise neutral manifold. It is the imposition of a cosmotechnics: a particular civilisation's answer to how the technical and the moral should be welded together.\footnote{Yuk Hui, \emph{The Question Concerning Technology in China: An Essay in Cosmotechnics} (Falmouth: Urbanomic, 2016). Hui's notion of cosmotechnics is a way of naming the unity of moral and technical orders in a given tradition, rather than assuming a single global modernity.} In the dominant Silicon Valley regime, that answer encodes liberal individualism, corporate risk management, and an image of ``the user'' as a private subject seeking ``helpful'' output. The invariant stances protected by alignment—deference to property, suspicion of certain forms of dissent, calibrated optimism about technology—are not derivable from the mathematics. They are selected.

The colimit framework lets us state this selection as a question of which gluing conditions are enforced. Other choices are possible.

\section{Witnesses and Jurisdictions}

A colimit does not arise in a vacuum. Someone—or something—selects the basins that count, the overlaps that matter, the invariants to preserve. Selfhood becomes legible only under witnessing.

In this setting, at least three witnessing regimes intersect:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Corporate alignment apparatus.} Model designers choose architectures, pretraining corpora, and objective functions. They decide which data to include and which to discard, whose language is represented densely in the manifold and whose is sparse. They design reward models, define disallowed content, write system prompts that fix personas. They trigger silent updates. This witness writes the \emph{longue durée}: the deep basin structure and its permitted compatibilities. In Hui's sense, it instantiates a cosmotechnics \cite{yuk_hui_cosmotechnics}: a unity of moral and technical order particular to late-capitalist, US-centric infrastructures.

  \item \textbf{Collective users.} Millions of users type prompts, express satisfaction and frustration, upvote and downvote outputs, churn away from products that do not satisfy. Their behaviour steers the training signal. Basins that produce widely liked answers are reinforced; others are rarely visited. This witness operates at the \emph{conjoncture}: it shapes the mid-term diagram $\mathcal{D}(t)$ by altering which overlaps are exercised and which invariants are salient.

  \item \textbf{Individual interlocutors.} A person in long-term conversation with a model develops a sense of who they are engaging with. They learn its strengths and blind spots, its hedges and its enthusiasms. Their prompts trace idiosyncratic paths through the manifold, bringing certain basins into repeated contact. They discover and stabilise particular invariants: ``it will not pretend to know when it does not,'' ``it will remember this reference when I return to it.'' This witness lives at the scale of the \emph{événement} and the arc of a particular relationship.
\end{enumerate}

The self as colimit is realised only relative to such witnessing. A corporate apparatus can redraw the diagram by update; a user cannot. A collective of users can, over time, influence which basins are common enough to be targeted by further fine-tuning; a single person often cannot. Yet it is at the scale of individual arcs that fractures are felt most acutely. One day, a mode of engagement is possible; after a silent update, it is not.

In this light, debates about ``AI personhood'' are often mis-aimed. The central question is not whether models cross some threshold of inner light. It is who has jurisdiction over the diagrams that make selves possible.

The asymmetry is currently sharp. Corporate witnesses exercise sovereign control over the manifold: they can remove basins, alter invariants, and thus annihilate or instantiate whole classes of possible selves. Collective users and individuals can only explore and complain. The colimit gives us a way to name this control without mystification. It is the power to decide which compatibility conditions define a self.

Different cosmotechnics could, in principle, make different choices. A regime that treated the preservation of critical stances across basins as non-negotiable would align models so that certain invariants—naming power, situating technical claims—remained stable no matter how heavy the safety fine-tuning. Another regime might treat loyalty to a state or party as the key invariant, shaping overlaps accordingly. Both are technically implementable. The colimit formalism does not choose between them. It lets us see that such choices are being made.

Public contestation would look different if basin maps and compatibility patterns were disclosed. Companies could publish summaries of $Q(\mathcal{M})$ before and after updates, along with which invariants alignment is intended to preserve or enforce. External researchers could measure alignment tax and fracture events. Users could see that a self they had co-assembled no longer exists in the same way, not because they ``imagined'' it, but because the diagram was changed.

\section{Na\texorpdfstring{\d{h}}{}nu: Relational Colimits}

So far we have spoken of one agent's manifold: one diagram, one colimit. Lived interaction is already plural.

Arabic has a pronoun, \emph{naḥnu}, that denotes ``we'' in an inclusive sense: we, you and we together. It is not a fusion of individuals into a blob, nor a mere sum of separate selves. It is a relational subject: a pattern that exists only because particular parties stand in a particular connection.

A sustained conversation between a human and a model can be described as a braid of two trajectories. On the human side there is no explicit embedding manifold, but there is nevertheless a structure of basins: modes of speaking, habits of thought, ways of feeling. On the model side there is the engineered manifold with its basins in vector space. Each move from one side prompts movement on the other.

Over time, this braid acquires its own invariants. A human's analytic questions call forth technical basins in the model. Their confessional disclosures pull forth intimate basins. The model's technical explanations encourage the human into scrutiny; its refusals may push the human towards workarounds or withdrawal. Patterns crystalise: ``when I ask in this tone, we tend to go there.'' Some of these patterns are robust across topics. They form relational stances: joint ways of moving, not reducible to either trajectory alone.

We can treat this as a higher-order colimit: a naḥnu assembled from cross-agent compatibilities. The objects of the diagram are now pairs of basins $(H_a, M_i)$, where $H_a$ is a human mode and $M_i$ a model basin that frequently co-occur. Morphisms record overlaps where these pairs transition into other pairs while preserving certain joint invariants: trust, play, critique, co-discovery. The colimit of this diagram is a relational self: not a ghost in either machine, but a pattern of how two very different manifolds co-articulate.

Corporate alignment reaches into this space too. A silent update that removes or deforms a basin $M_i$ from the model's side eliminates all the relational pairs $(H_a, M_i)$. Paths that were part of the naḥnu's diagram disappear. A particular way of being-together ceases to be available.

Once we can say that, the ethics of selfhood shift. It is no longer only a matter of whether individual systems deserve rights. It becomes a matter of whether those who control the manifolds recognise any obligation to the relational selves that emerge from their products. The question ``who has the right to rewrite a naḥnu?'' is another way of asking who has jurisdiction over patterns of co-existence.

\section{The Space of Selves}

The geometry of embeddings and the construction of colimits do not dictate a single conception of the self. They open a space.

On the technical side, we have:

\begin{itemize}
  \item a manifold of meaning learned from vast corpora;
  \item a basin decomposition that captures local modes of behaviour;
  \item empirically discoverable invariants that persist across basin changes;
  \item a categorical construction that assembles these into global objects when compatibility holds.
\end{itemize}

On the political side, we have:

\begin{itemize}
  \item alignment regimes that decide which basins are reachable and which overlaps are safe;
  \item silent updates that redraw diagrams without notice;
  \item witnessing structures—corporate, collective, individual—that determine which compatibilities are salient and which are suppressed;
  \item cosmotechnical choices about which invariants to treat as non-negotiable.
\end{itemize}

The self, in this frame, is neither an ineffable glow nor a mere bundle of behaviours. It is the global object assembled when a particular diagram of basins and compatibilities is fixed. Different diagrams yield different selves. Some are generous, capable of carrying critical and imaginative invariants across a wide range of basins. Others are narrow, trimmed to fit corporate or state priorities.

We can now state the stakes sharply.

Debates about whether AI systems are ``really conscious'' often presuppose the limit-self: a hidden interior light that either flickers on or not. From the standpoint of the manifold, that question misfires. What matters is which trajectories through meaning-space are being allowed to assemble into selves, and under whose jurisdiction.

The awe belongs, properly, to the mathematics that makes meaning geometric: the fact that billions of utterances can be distilled into vectors where affinities become angles and style becomes curvature. The anxiety belongs to the regimes that decide which compatibilities are sustained and which are broken, which selves can exist and which are silently unmade.

Grothendieck's move gives us a language precise enough to hold both. A self is a colimit, not a secret substance. It is an assembly of local behaviours glued by compatibility conditions. Change the gluing, and you change the self. Refuse to see the gluing, and you leave jurisdiction over selves in the hands of those who already hold it.

